From: Vadim Deiss <letechnarb@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nss_core: reset truesize when reusing a tx skb as a receive buffer

nss_skb_reuse() clears the sk_buff up to tail. truesize lies past tail,
so a reused buffer keeps the truesize of its previous life.

Since 238e03d04662 ("net: fix memory leak in skb_segment_list for GRO
packets", CVE-2026-22979, in 6.18), skb_segment_list() no longer
subtracts the segments' truesize from the head: a fraglist GRO head
keeps the truesize of all its segments. When such a head is sent through
NSS with reuse and comes back as a receive buffer, the inflated value is
carried over, and the next fraglist GRO merge adds to it again. The value
grows over reuse cycles; tens of MB were observed on received linear
skbs whose fresh truesize is 4352.

At 2^31:
- a UDP socket on 6.18 dies for good: on an empty queue
  __udp_enqueue_schedule_skb() accepts the skb and adds truesize to
  udp_prod_queue->rmem_alloc, but udp_skb_truesize() masks bit 31
  (UDP_SKB_IS_STATELESS), so the drain subtracts 2^31 less and
  rmem_alloc stays at 0x80000000;
- IPv4 reassembly stops for good: ip_frag_queue() adds the unsigned
  truesize to fqdir->mem, inet_frag_reasm_finish() subtracts an int sum,
  leaving FRAG memory at 2^32 (kuncy7/openwrt-nss-edma#54).

Reset truesize to what a fresh buffer of that size gets, as
napi_reuse_skb() does since e33d0ba8047b ("net-gro: reset skb->truesize
in napi_reuse_skb()").

Link: https://github.com/kuncy7/openwrt-nss-edma/issues/54
Signed-off-by: Vadim Deiss <letechnarb@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/nss_core.c b/nss_core.c
index f01f14c..616fc14 100644
--- a/nss_core.c
+++ b/nss_core.c
@@ -2894,6 +2894,16 @@ void nss_skb_reuse(struct sk_buff *nbuf)
 	skb_reset_tail_pointer(nbuf);
 
 	nbuf->head_frag = head_frag;
+
+	/*
+	 * truesize lies past tail, so the memset above keeps the value from
+	 * the skb's previous life. The stack may have grown it (a fraglist
+	 * GRO head keeps the truesize of all its segments after
+	 * skb_segment_list()), and it would then accumulate over reuse cycles.
+	 * Reset it to what a fresh buffer of this size gets, as
+	 * napi_reuse_skb() does.
+	 */
+	nbuf->truesize = SKB_TRUESIZE(skb_end_offset(nbuf));
 }
 #endif
 
